```
Here are two alternative proposals for your consideration:

**(1)** Use `for file` or `for all`. It's common in Twig to use two words like `ignore missing`, `with ... only`, `as macros`, etc.

```twig
{% extends 'layout.html.twig' %}
{% trans_default_domain 'Blog' for file %}
{% trans_default_domain 'Blog' for all %}

<h1>{{ 'page.headline'|trans }}</h1>

<twig:Blog:Card>
    {{ 'promotion.headline'|trans }}
</twig:Blog:Card>
```

**(2)** Add a new `trans_domain` tag to replace `trans_default_domain`:

```twig
{% extends 'layout.html.twig' %}
{% trans_domain 'Blog' %}

<h1>{{ 'page.headline'|trans }}</h1>

<twig:Blog:Card>
    {{ 'promotion.headline'|trans }}
</twig:Blog:Card>
```

We could use the same tag inside embeds/components to override the global one:

```twig
{% extends 'layout.html.twig' %}
{% trans_domain 'Blog' %}

{# ... #}

<twig:Blog:Card>
    {% trans_domain 'User' %}
    {{ 'promotion.headline'|trans }}
</twig:Blog:Card>
```

This new tag could add an `only` modifier (same as `include ... only`) to apply the current `trans_default_domain` behavior and apply the trans domain only to the main page contents, not embeds or components:

```twig
{% extends 'layout.html.twig' %}
{% trans_domain 'Blog' only %}

{# ... #}
```

