by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-02T07:23:01Z

Two more commits, implementing the deprecation path I sketched above. Happy to drop them from this PR and move them to a separate one if you prefer to keep the feature clean, but I'd rather we settle the target semantics now.

**The 9.0 model they encode**, at the top level of a template:

* a **constant** `trans_default_domain` becomes a declaration for the whole template, i.e. what `for _self` does today: one per template, before any `embed`, applying to everything written in the source;
* a **dynamic** one is unchanged and keeps applying from the point where it is declared. It can't be inlined into the separately compiled class of an `embed` body, so it can't be a template-wide property. Nothing about it is deprecated.

Deprecated, all constant-only, all triggered only when they'd actually change something:

* the tag inside a `macro`, which is #64001: the visitor's scope follows traversal order, not lexical position, so the domain leaks into the macros declared after it and misses the ones before. Scoped to the enclosing macro in 9.0;
* a top-level declaration in a template that also has an `embed` whose body holds a domain-less `trans` call;
* a domain-less `trans` call preceding the declaration;
* more than one top-level declaration.

Also in there: `for _self` inside a macro is now a `SyntaxError`. It was accepted and silently applied to the whole template, a macro isn't a `BlockNode` so the block-depth guard didn't catch it.

Known imprecision, flagging it rather than papering over it: the last two fire per `ModuleNode`, and an `embed` body is its own module, so the odd template that puts two top-level declarations inside one `embed` body gets deprecated even though embed bodies keep positional semantics in 9.0. There's no way to tell "this module is an embed body" during traversal, the enclosing one is traversed last and `index` is only set afterwards. Deferring emission to the parent's `leaveNode` would fix it but the outermost module has no parent to defer to. Both shapes are rare and the suggested fix still applies, so I left it.

Side note, unrelated and pre-existing on 8.2: a block whose only content is the tag doesn't compile, `{% block a %}{% trans_default_domain 'x' %}{% endblock %}` gives `Node "body" does not exist for Node "Twig\Node\BlockNode"`, because the visitor removes the node and leaves the block empty.

