by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-18T13:46:12Z

I split the deprecations out and pushed the feature on its own, rebased on 8.2.

The reason for splitting: one of the four deprecations cannot be delivered as announced. For `{% embed %}...{% endembed %}{% trans_default_domain 'D' %}`, the message tells you to add `for _self`, but doing that throws `The "trans_default_domain" tag must be used with "for _self" before any "embed" tag`. And the 9.0 semantics the deprecation announces, a top-level declaration applying to the whole template, cannot be reached for that shape through this mechanism, so 9.0 would have to turn it into a syntax error, which the deprecation does not say. That needs its own discussion, and it should not hold up the feature.

I also fixed one bug. `TranslationDefaultDomainNodeVisitor::$scope` is instance state, pushed on entering a `ModuleNode` and popped on leaving it, so when a template throws the stack is never unwound and the domain survives into the next template compiled by the same `Environment`. That matters for `cache:warmup` and for dev-mode recompiles, where many templates go through one `Environment`. Before the fix, compiling a broken template and then an unrelated one gave:

```
caught: The "trans_default_domain" tag cannot be used with "for _self" inside a block in "bad" at line 1.
INNOCENT[from-D]      <- expected INNOCENT[from-messages]
```

A `ModuleNode` is always the root of a traversal, and embedded templates are traversed as separate top-level passes rather than nested inside the parent, so entering one now starts from a fresh `Scope`. There is a regression test.

Smaller things: the changelog entry moved from 8.1 to 8.2, and I dropped two unrelated hunks in `TranslationExtensionTest` that removed a trailing period from the expected messages of `testTransUnknownKeyword` and `testTransComplexBody`, since 8.2 passes with the period. The nine commits are squashed into one, with you as the author.

The rest verifies. I checked the scoping matrix against 8.2 for top level, inside a block, `extends`, `include`, `embed`, nested `embed` and macros, and there is no leakage: an `include`d template keeps `messages`, a parent body keeps `messages`, a separate template embedded in a `for _self` template keeps its own domain, and an `embed` body declaring its own domain wins. Extraction agrees with rendering, which is the part I most expected to be wrong: a `for _self` fixture yields `[Blog] promotion.headline, page.headline, page.intro, macro.key`, `[Other] explicit.key` and `[messages] card.default`.

